    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d839">

                <pb facs="1228" n="1214" sameAs="frus:frus1947v05/pg1214"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.BB Palestine/10–2847</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Near Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Merriam</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Director of the Office of Near Eastern and African Affairs</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Henderson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1947-10-28">October 28, 1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>New York has revised the detailed position paper on the UNSCOP majority
                        report<anchor n="1" xml:id="d839-f1"/> to “bring it in line” with our
                    proposal that the transition period be shortened to July 1, 1948.</p>
                <pb facs="1229" n="1215" xml:id="pg1215"/>
                <p>Actually you will see from Miss McCown’s memo attached<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d839-f2"/> that New York has gone much further than a mere revision
                    to conform to a shorter transitional period.</p>
                <p>Inasmuch as our recent experience indicates that New York, in requesting our
                    comments seems to expect a rubber stamp, and ignores even the most strenuous
                    objections, it would appear useless to send any detailed revisions. If you
                    approve I will inform Mr. Wilkins what our reaction is in general to the
                    revisions, but leave it to New York to make any detailed revision, if they
                    should desire to give consideration to our objections.<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d839-f3"/>
                </p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d839-f1" n="1" xml:id="d839-f1-content">
                <p>See memorandum of October 24, <ref target="#pg1202">p. 1202</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d839-f2" n="2" xml:id="d839-f2-content">
                <p>Undated memorandum by D. Beatrice McCown of the Division of Near Eastern Affairs,
                    not printed. The memorandum began with: “The Palestine Committee of the US
                    Delegation to the UN appears to have felt itself completely free to ignore the
                    revisions suggested by, and approved in, the Department with respect to the
                    Majority Plan. In fact, they are proposing changes which would be so far
                    reaching in ultimate effect in Palestine that a major policy decision is
                    involved.” The substance of Miss McCown’s analysis in the remainder of the
                    memorandum was incorporated in Mr. Merriam’s letter of October 30 to Mr.
                    Wilkins, <ref target="#pg1217">p. 1217</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d839-f3" n="3" xml:id="d839-f3-content">
                <p>Mr. Henderson expressed his approval in a marginal notation.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
